# The Color Side Quest and Finding the Right Angle

**Visual Composition, Gamifying Life, and the Joy of Casual Art**

I hesitate to even call photography a "hobby" in the traditional sense, at least not the way I do it. I don't go out on dedicated photo walks with a tripod. I just happen to be someone who stops in the middle of the street because the lighting hitting a building looks cool, or because the sky is doing something interesting.

For me, taking pictures is just a low-stakes way to train my eye. It is all about visual composition—which actually feeds directly back into my other hobbies. Figuring out how to frame a good photo helps me understand how to frame a scene in Roblox Studio, or how to set up atmospheric lighting in a game engine.

## I. Gamifying Real Life: The Color Side Quest

One of the most fun things I've done with photography wasn't a formal project, but a random "side quest" I did with some friends.

We decided to pick a single, specific color, and our objective was to go out and only take pictures that heavily featured that exact color. It sounds stupidly simple, but it completely changed the way I looked at my environment. Suddenly, you aren't just walking down a street; you are scanning for visual anchors. It turned real life into an IRL fetch quest. It forced me to look at textures, shadows, and objects I would normally completely ignore, just to capture that specific aesthetic.

## II. The "Non-Existent" Hobby

There is this weird pressure on the internet that if you enjoy doing something, you have to immediately monetize it, master it, or buy expensive gear for it. (seriously, tiktok?) Photography is my deliberate escape from that.

I don't need to be a professional to care about aesthetics. Sometimes, art doesn't need a deeper meaning or a steep learning curve. Sometimes, the only justification you need to take a picture is, "This looked really pretty, and I wanted to save it."

## III. The Albums

Below, you won't find highly curated, award-winning gallery shots. What you will find are albums of my favorite moments, experiments with color grading, and a few artifacts from our color-themed side quests. It's just a collection of life, documented.
